{"reply":"What may be catching you is not simply the mistake, but the effort to make it disappear before anyone can react. The gardeners repaint the roses because discovery could bring severe punishment—and the scene ultimately undercuts their hope of keeping it hidden: the Queen notices and orders their execution. Your note has a much less extreme situation, but it contains a related sequence: you fixed the billing mistake and then said nothing.

Research on error reporting suggests that speaking up depends partly on perceived interpersonal risk and psychological safety: people are more willing to report errors when they expect openness and support, and less willing when reporting feels threatening. That research is about hospital nursing teams, so it can illuminate a possible mechanism without proving what caused your silence or implying that your situation was dangerous. [Study on error reporting and team dynamics](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/)

So perhaps your reaction is an uneasy recognition of the gap between “the error is fixed” and “the error is acknowledged.” You might ask yourself: was the hardest part the mistake, the possibility of someone’s response, or the fact that fixing it privately let you move on without having to be seen in the mistake?","evidence_uses":[{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln1931-1970","source_location":"Chapter 8 — The Queen’s Croquet-Ground, source lines 1931-1970","supported_claims":["The gardeners repaint the roses because discovery could bring severe punishment."]},{"source_kind":"book_corpus","evidence_id":"pg11-v01b38ea4-ch08-ln2018-2074","source_location":"Chapter 8 — The Queen’s Croquet-Ground, source lines 2018-2074","supported_claims":["The scene ultimately undercuts their hope of keeping it hidden: the Queen notices and orders their execution."]},{"source_kind":"memory","evidence_id":"mem_0594635eea06bc168ee40cba58375355ae4a67f9a321234741a55ad230294112","supported_claims":["Your note has a much less extreme situation, but it contains a related sequence: you fixed the billing mistake and then said nothing."]},{"source_kind":"web","evidence_id":"https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC10599306/","supported_claims":["Research on error reporting suggests that speaking up depends partly on perceived interpersonal risk and psychological safety: people are more willing to report errors when they expect openness and support, and less willing when reporting feels threatening.","That research is about hospital nursing teams, so it can illuminate a possible mechanism without proving what caused your silence or implying that your situation was dangerous."],"exact_quote":null}],"memory":{"kind":"no_memory_candidate","reason_code":"automatic_capture_disabled"}}

